# Review package: batch 6, slice mode-delta

## 5e20285c Stop what a leader does as soon as the app leaves lead, and let no read write

Batch 6's review of the app's modes (runs/batch6-mode-r2-agy.md) found work that crosses a step-down. Every claim held, and
each fix below came from a test seen red for that reason.

A read no longer writes: subViews and machineNodes stop expiring stale nodes, so the five screens cannot answer 500 under
plan 3's fence. The minute round's reconcile stays the one writer. A waiting update stops on leaving lead: its 30 s timer,
bus subscription, held turns and hand-over timeout go, nothing is pumped, and a POST whose registry read crossed a step-down
is refused before it reaches the updater. A socket that crossed a step-down is refused on all three socket routes, and open
sockets close in the very call that leaves lead. A stay in lead that ended stops its remaining hooks and leaves only those it
entered. The entry sweeps and minute rounds run one at a time, and leaving waits for what runs. A hook registered late waits
for what is pending. freshState refuses a word from the future. A second start and a late check after stop do nothing, and
the hook log line goes through loggable.

The 2 s promise is now shown on a fake clock with fromKeeper true, and that test was seen to fail with the poll at 3000 ms.
The socket test keeps the real path from the keeper's word to the socket, with no timer of its own.

Ruling: refused a crossing socket with a second modeGate in preHandler, not on the connection event - why: connection fires after the upgrade and the route's handler still runs right after it and reaches the hub and the terminals; at preHandler the client gets the same 503 and nothing waits between it and the upgrade - cost if wrong: a route that later adds its own waiting preHandler reopens the window, and its socket would stay open until the next step-down.
Ruling: expireStaleNodes left the read paths in every mode, not only outside lead - why: only the leader writes and a read must not, and a 24 hour limit does not need the last minute; socket.test.ts sweeps before its two reads, as the read did before - cost if wrong: a screen shows a stale node as ready up to a minute longer.
Ruling: AppMode gains whenLeaving; whenLead, current, settled, start, stop and whileLeading keep their names and shapes - why: closing sockets and standing down the update cannot wait behind an enter hook, and must run even when their hook never entered - cost if wrong: one more method to know.
Ruling: a hook registered while leading is entered after what is pending, not synchronously, against plan 2's "entered at once" - why: the review's item 6, hooks keep their registered order - cost if wrong: a caller that reads the hook's effect right after whenLead must await settled().
Ruling: freshState allows no skew - why: keeper and app share one host's clock, so a word ahead of now means the clock stepped back and says nothing about a live keeper; null keeps the mode and a live keeper's next word is obeyed - cost if wrong: a word written between reading now and reading the file waits one more check, a second.
Ruling: a tick that finds a sweep running is dropped, not chained - why: the next tick does the same work with a fresh now, as sendWaiting and reconcile already skip themselves - cost if wrong: a round is late by up to one tick after an overrun.
Ruling: a newer hand-over replaces the company's older release timeout, instead of both running - why: leaving lead has to clear them, so they are kept by company - cost if wrong: turns stay held up to ten minutes from the newest hand-over, not the oldest.
Ruling: updateRoutes' onClose hook is gone - why: stop() leaves lead, which runs standDown, and an app not in lead has nothing waiting - cost if wrong: none seen; a waiting update on a closing app that never led cannot exist.
Ruling: a links.sync in flight when the app leaves lead may still write tunnel.json, left as it is - why: the review's fix names it but this round's item 4 does not, and by plan 2 the keeper reads the link from the database while tunnel.json is the updater's and installer's copy of the same row - cost if wrong: the copy is written once after a step-down, with what the database holds.

